WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.920
The Conjuring 3 is chock full of easter eggs&nbsp;
from secret Annabelle and Nun connections to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.160
classic horror movie tributes and clues&nbsp;
to the movie's big twists and reveals.
&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.080
Yippee ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and&nbsp;
in this video I'm revealing all the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.440
you might have missed in The Devil Made&nbsp;
Me Do It and explaining how the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.840
connects to the wider Conjuring universe.
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.
&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.120
The creepy Annabelle doll is an iconic figure&nbsp;
in the Conjuring franchise and The Devil Made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.960
Me Do It expands on the story behind the&nbsp;
satanic cult "Disciples of the Ram".
&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.160
In the Conjuring 3, Ed and Lorraine meet&nbsp;
Father Kastner, a priest who investigated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.160
and exposed "The Disciples of the Ram".
Kastner tells the Warrens they shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.480
underestimate the power of these Satanists,&nbsp;
and when Ed asks him why someone would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.080
target an innocent child...
"The "why" is irrelevant.
&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.840
The "why" is counter to everything&nbsp;
that the Satanist stands for.
&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.640
His sole aim is chaos."
...echoes what Detective Clarkin told the Forms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.880
Annabelle about what he'd learned about the cult.
"That they did this to prove their devotion.
&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.360
A sort of violence for violence's sake."
While researching the Disciples of the Ram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.640
Kastner collected various occult objects and&nbsp;
books, and kept them locked in his basement room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.960
similar to the Warrens' artefact room where&nbsp;
they keep the Annabelle doll and many cursed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.760
and haunted objects from their investigations.
And his answer to Lorraine's suggestion that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.760
should burn all of it...
"I've thought of it.
&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.480
But I felt it was safer to keep them locked.
I like taking guns off the streets."
&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.440
...is similar to what Ed said in&nbsp;
The Conjuring when we were first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.920
introduced to the Warrens' Artefact room.
"Why not just throw them in an incinerator?"
&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.480
"Well, that would only destroy the vessel.
Sometimes it's better to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.120
the genie in the bottle.
It's kind of like keeping guns off the street."
&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.800
And there's another Conjuring easter egg&nbsp;
in The Devil Made Me Do It as the Perrons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.560
the family from the first movie's haunted&nbsp;
house, are name-dropped when the Warrens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.640
receive some Get Well Soon flowers.
"Look what the Perron family sent.
&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.640
Oh, that was sweet of them."
The demon nun Valak was the main antagonist in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.440
Conjuring 2 and although she didn't make a direct&nbsp;
appearance in the The Devil Made Me Do It, after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.040
Arne's been possessed there's a creepy little Nun&nbsp;
figurine in his bedroom at the boarding kennels.
&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.840
Also in the movie's epilogue scenes is the&nbsp;
painting of the nun that came alive in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.960
second Conjuring movie and which has now been&nbsp;
moved into the Artefact room for safekeeping.
&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.880
However, notice how the painting is&nbsp;
kept framed in the shot as Ed brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.600
the Occultist's cursed chalice into the room,
reminding us that Valak is still the principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.400
villain of the franchise and&nbsp;
lurking in the background,
&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.440
just like Annabelle who is also carefully&nbsp;
framed in shot when the camera switches angles.
&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.280
By the way, there was also another little&nbsp;
nod to The Conjuring 2, when Lorraine sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.280
an upside down cross while searching for&nbsp;
the Occultist's location, referencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.840
the chilling scene where Janet sees all the&nbsp;
crosses in a room slowly turn upside down.
&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.320
A similar moment happens in the opening of&nbsp;
The Nun where a crucifix slowly inverts as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.960
Valak emerges from the depths of the abbey,
and in the Conjuring Universe inverted crosses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.120
are an demonic indicator, such as the one&nbsp;
that appears on Frenchie's neck in the Nun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.640
and which the Warrens talk about during&nbsp;
their presentation about possession. 
&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.360
"And like that, an upside-down cross&nbsp;
started to appear from within his body."
&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.320
Valak, of course, got her own evil backstory&nbsp;
in The Nun movie, and a connection with The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.880
Conjuring 3 that you might not have noticed is&nbsp;
that one of the Occultist's victims, Jessica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.880
is played by actress Ingrid Bisu, who also starred&nbsp;
as Sister Oana in the demon nun's origin story.
&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.040
The fact these two characters are played&nbsp;
by the same actor could throw up some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.720
interesting theories about the Conjuring Universe,&nbsp;
especially given that spoiler alert for the Nun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.920
Sister Oana was already dead in that movie.
Just before Arne murders Bruno,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.760
take a closer look at the two posters&nbsp;
on the wall behind his landlord. 
&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.480
First of all, there's Black Sabbath's iconic&nbsp;
album cover featuring a mysterious – even maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.000
witch-like – woman, a reference to the Occultist&nbsp;
who is manipulating Arne in this very moment;
&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.800
and then there's Ella Fitzgerald's Mack The&nbsp;
Knife, the famous song about a legendary criminal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.040
which foreshadows how Arne is about to pick&nbsp;
up a knife and become a notorious killer.
&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.120
There are a number of books you can see&nbsp;
behind Ed and Lorraine in the office of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.800
Arne's defence attorney, but one of&nbsp;
particular relevance is "Jonathan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.840
Strange &amp; Mr Norrell" by Susanna Clarke,
because the novel is set in an alternate history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.440
of 19th-century England, much like how the third&nbsp;
Conjuring movie puts its own fictionalised spin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.000
on real-life events, giving us an alternate&nbsp;
history of the Trial of Arne Johnson.
&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:36.320
In The Conjuring 3, director Michael Chaves wanted&nbsp;
to pay homage to several iconic horror movies.
&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.960
So at the beginning of the film when Father Gordon&nbsp;
arrives in a cab at the Glatzel family home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.880
with a hat, coat and briefcase, the&nbsp;
mist and black silhouette figure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.200
he forms is a homage to the scene where&nbsp;
Father Merrin arrives in The Exorcist.
&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.840
Other references to the iconic film include how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.080
David wears speckled blue pyjamas&nbsp;
during the exorcism like Regan.
&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.640
And how Arne sacrifices himself for David&nbsp;
by inviting the demon into his body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.960
similar to Father Karras in the The Exorcist,&nbsp;
although the outcome, of course, is different. 
&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.600
Chaves also says that the figure of Arne&nbsp;
appearing in a window as Father Gordon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.240
arrives is a deliberate reference to Janet Leigh&nbsp;
"seeing Mother in the window" when she first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.800
arrives at the Bates Motel in Psycho.
And the shot of David being showered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.840
blood inside the bathtub may also be a nod to&nbsp;
the iconic shower scene in that same film.
&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.360
The waterbed scene in The Devil Made Me Do It is&nbsp;
also another deliberate tribute by the director to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.480
one of the first horror movies he ever saw, "A&nbsp;
Nightmare on Elm Street 4: The Dream Master".
&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.480
So when David senses a face appearing&nbsp;
to him from inside the mattress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.440
it's similar to how Joey in Elm Street 4&nbsp;
sees a model appear to him inside the bed.
&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.160
And the hand bursting out of the mattress&nbsp;
to grab David is a nod to how Freddy Krueger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.280
jumps out of the waterbed to attack Joey.
When Ed is possessed by the Occultist while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.360
he's searching for Lorraine in the tunnels,
the shots of him holding a hammer and dragging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.680
his injured leg along are a little hat-tip to&nbsp;
the showdown at the end of The Shining where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.880
Jack Torrance limps along with an axe, and is,&nbsp;
like Ed, being influenced by an evil entity.
&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.680
There's an Annabelle easter egg via the&nbsp;
name of the Palmeri Funeral Home which Ed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.240
and Lorraine visit to try and make a connection&nbsp;
with the Occultist through Jessica's dead body.
&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.840
Palmeri was the name of the Pasadena&nbsp;
apartment complex that Mia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.600
John moved to in the first Annabelle.
"You're the new tenants at the Palmeri."
&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.360
It's also the surname of Mary Ellen's crush&nbsp;
in Annabelle Comes Home, Bob Palmeri.
&nbsp;

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.160
"Bob's-Got-Balls.
How you doing?"
&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.920
There's also a visual shout out to&nbsp;
Annabelle Comes Home via the creepy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.880
colour wheel that has been added&nbsp;
to the Warrens' artefact room.
&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.080
The wheel terrorised Judy Warren in that movie&nbsp;
when it manifested the many forms Annabelle has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.080
taken in the Conjuring franchise.
"I like your dolls."
&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.840
Although I prefer previous Conjuring&nbsp;
villains like Valak and Bathsheba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.400
the Occultist has some interesting paranormal&nbsp;
powers such as the ability to manipulate her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.520
victims from afar and create optical illusions&nbsp;
to trick them into acts they wouldn't do.
&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.400
She made Arne Johnson think his landlord was&nbsp;
some kind of hellish creature attacking him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.040
and also made Ed Warren momentarily&nbsp;
think Lorraine was an evil monster.
&nbsp;

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:14.400
The Occultist had a copy of Lorraine appear in&nbsp;
front of her, mirroring her movements exactly.
&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.680
This trippy moment was foreshadowed&nbsp;
at the beginning of the movie during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.760
David's exorcism when the camera that&nbsp;
Drew was filming with was knocked over.
&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:25.760
As the camera continued to film, it was&nbsp;
pointed towards a TV that was screening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.480
the live recording, causing an image of the&nbsp;
screen to replicate itself endlessly. 
&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:35.520
This was a neat visual piece of foreshadowing that&nbsp;
was originally going to lead in to an even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.920
trippy sequence than we got in the final movie.
In the trailers for the movie, you can see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:44.240
deleted scene where Lorraine sees many&nbsp;
copies of herself, replicating endlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.497
in a spiral of sorts into the distance.
And if you want to learn more about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.497 --> 00:07:46.582
and all the other deleted scenes including&nbsp;
a cut post-credits scene, I'll be talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

